This is a readme fine for the prompt:
https://github.com/araman1-dev/AEO-Score-Agent.git
I would like to create a new project called AEO-Score-Agent in my Github. In this project, I would like to create an Agent that when invoked will ask for the link to a website and when the user enters the URL, then the agent will need to do a few core things: crawl a given URL (and its subpages), parse the HTML, inspect structured data, analyze content quality, and use libraries like requests, BeautifulSoup or scrapy for crawling, extruct or json-ld parsers for schema extraction, and optionally an LLM call to evaluate content quality and conversational ton and then score each factor. 
Here are the details of the AEO Readiness Checklist & Scoring Framework:

Below is a checklist organized into categories. Each item has a suggested weight so you can roll up a final score of 1–10.

1. Structured Data / Schema Markup (25 points)
CheckPointsFAQ schema present on relevant pages5HowTo schema present where applicable4Speakable schema present (voice assistant readiness)4Article or NewsArticle schema on blog/content pages3Organization or LocalBusiness schema on homepage3BreadcrumbList schema present2Product/Review schema (if e-commerce)2No schema validation errors (test via Google Rich Results)2

2. Content Structure & Answer Readiness (25 points)
CheckPointsPages contain clear, direct answers near the top of the content5Use of question-based H2/H3 headers (e.g., "What is X?" "How do I Y?")5Concise paragraph answers (40–60 words) following each question header5FAQ sections present on key pages4Use of numbered lists and step-by-step formatting for process content3Content uses natural, conversational language (not just keyword-stuffed)3

3. E-E-A-T Signals (20 points)
CheckPointsAuthor bylines present with bio/credentials4Author schema markup linking to author profile3Clear "About Us" page with organization credentials3Citations, sources, or external links to authoritative references3Content includes publication and last-updated dates4Trust signals present (awards, certifications, partnerships)3

4. Technical & Metadata (15 points)
CheckPointsPage titles are clear and question/answer-friendly3Meta descriptions are concise and written as direct answers3Canonical tags properly set2Pages load fast (under 3 seconds)3Mobile-friendly / responsive design2HTTPS enabled2

5. Semantic & NLP Readiness (10 points)
CheckPointsContent covers topic entities thoroughly (not just target keywords)3Use of natural language question phrasing throughout content3Internal linking connects related topics logically2Alt text on images is descriptive and contextual2

6. Voice Search Readiness (5 points)
CheckPointsSpeakable schema identifies the most answer-worthy passages2Content is written at a conversational reading level (aim for Grade 8 or below)2Short, direct sentences used in key answer sections1

Scoring Conversion (Total out of 100 → Score of 1–10)
Just divide the total raw score by 10 to get your 1–10 AEO readiness score. For example, a site that scores 73 out of 100 gets a 7.3 AEO score.
You can also add letter grades:

9–10: AEO-optimized
7–8.9: Good, minor gaps
5–6.9: Moderate readiness, significant room to improve
3–4.9: Low readiness
1–2.9: Not AEO-ready

Implementation Tips
For automated checks, most of the structured data, metadata, and technical items can be fully automated with code. For content quality checks (conversational tone, answer directness, E-E-A-T signals), you'll get much better results by passing the extracted page text to an LLM and asking it to score those specific dimensions. That hybrid approach — code for the technical layer, LLM for the content layer — will give you the most accurate and actionable scores. 
